e a detached gradient-enabled feature proxy, and process chunks of valid positions, keeping all 129 candidates within each chunk. Backpropagate each chunk’s summed loss divided by the batch’s total valid-position count. Accumulate output-embedding gradients and proxy gradients, then backpropagate the proxy gradient through the encoder once. Clip gradients, if the baseline does, only after both paths accumulate, and step once. Tied item embeddings must receive both scoring and encoder gradients. Fix sampled IDs and compare losses and every parameter gradient with a tiny unchunked reference. Separate softmax denominators across negative chunks would change the objective.\n\nBefore running, preregister baseline, objective-only A and combined B, plus two fusion controls under A: parameter-matched gating of h without recent pooling, and an ungated recent residual with zero-initialized scale. Retain the baseline optimizer and batch settings; record their resolved values with all configuration and software versions. Fix validation cadence to every five completed epochs plus the terminal checkpoint, with earliest checkpoint winning ties. Use the same checkpoint rule for every configuration: maximal four-category macro validation HR@10 across synchronized category checkpoints. Allocate at most 30,000 aggregate GPU-seconds to these five configurations, at most 6,000 each, including their diagnostics and debugging; reserve 13,200 for failed-run recovery and confirmation. Log actual GPU-seconds, including failures, and stop before the shared 43,200 limit. Four slots alter concurrency, not this allowance. Budget truncation can produce unequal completed epochs; report those counts. Do not launch additional window or gate sweeps without revising and charging the fixed allocation. Reusing validation for confirmation does not provide independent evidence.\n\nUse only the pinned validation protocol: 200 users per category, 99 unique negative candidates, evaluation seed 42. Report each category and macro HR@10. Where the authorized evaluator exposes per-user hits, store paired indicators and bootstrap users jointly across models within each category, averaging the four category differences for 95% intervals. These intervals condition on fixed training seeds and candidates, exclude training variability and do not correct adaptive-selection bias. One changed hit changes category HR by 0.5 percentage points and macro HR by 0.125. Retain the baseline for inconclusive gains; never substitute diagnostic margins or simulator judgments for selection. Full-catalog unseen-item recommendations for twenty training users remain qualitative diagnostics, not the benchmark metric. Popularity and recent-item affinity may be shortcuts; inspect training-frequency and history-length strata descriptively without adding selection criteria or unavailable trait labels.\n\nSave the architecture configuration, residual parameters, category mappings and checkpoints. Update the predictor writer so working/predict.py reconstructs the actual trained model and uses identical causal pooling and scoring. Preserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). view.model must be the trained nn.Module; log2feats must expose fused features and predict_score must use the trained scoring path. Handle loader tuples (None, sequence, positive, negative), each tensor B×L. Return metrics and metric_definitions; the interpreter prompt must use those measured values and definitions. Preserve user_train, meta_data and review_data compatibility without treating unavailable reviews, ratings, item categories, prices, stores or simulator traits as observed. Use only public histories, timestamps, titles and descriptions; omit unavailable category breakdowns or mark them unavailable rather than interpreting empty counts.",
