INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 1: The recommended first experiment is **recent-context gated SASRec with uniform multi-negative training**. It targets stale historical influence and broadly plausible but weak immediate matches, while testing the observed ranking-separation problem. Improvements remain unverified.

### Insights and directions

**Starting-point insights.** (1) SASRec already represents ordered context through causal attention. A small residual branch can therefore test recency without replacing the backbone. (2) Users report historical interests overwhelming a specific current purpose. This motivates explicitly balancing recent and longer context, although recent purchases do not establish continuing demand. (3) Electronics has 14.95% sampled wrong ranks and a −0.4812 tenth-percentile margin. These measurements motivate stronger discrimination, but do not establish validation HR@10.

**Related-work insights.** (1) MSMT-LSI and Mixture of Sequence distinguish temporal scales. They motivate a lightweight gate, without establishing gains on these datasets. (2) gSASRec and eSASRec motivate multi-negative objectives. Their findings do not isolate the best loss for this evaluator. (3) Enhanced BPR warns about false negatives during hard mining. Uniform negatives with observed-positive exclusion provide a simpler initial experiment.

### Framework

| Direction | Existing mechanism | Proposed minimal test | Remaining gap |
|---|---|---|---|
| Temporal relevance | Multiscale encoders | Recent-pool residual gate | Recency is not verified intent |
| Ranking discrimination | Multi-negative losses | Uniform sampled softmax | Unobserved positives remain |
| Functional relevance | Separate transition/text branches | Defer initially | Transitions do not verify compatibility |

### Candidate assessment

Scores are originality / potential / user alignment / model alignment / implementation difficulty; higher difficulty means harder. Each entry gives a positive and negative assessment for every dimension.

| Idea | Scores | Positive / negative assessments |
|---|---|---|
| A: recent-context gate | 3/6/8/4/2 | O: focused adaptation / familiar fusion; P: extensible / limited capacity; U: targets stale interests / misses compatibility; M: tests context ambiguity / weak separation effect uncertain; D: small addition / masking needed |
| B: multi-negative softmax | 2/7/4/9/3 | O: targeted objective / established method; P: reusable / sampling sensitive; U: sharper ranking / no explicit recency; M: targets wrong ranks / diagnostic transfer uncertain; D: batched scoring / memory cost |
| **C: A+B** | **4/8/8/9/4** | O: coupled intervention / known components; P: separable ablations / interactions complicate attribution; U: current-purpose ranking / cannot verify prerequisites; M: stronger discrimination / false negatives persist; D: modest changes / predictor parity required |

### Selected experiment

At 0% progress, choose C as a small, ablatable extension. Fuse the causal SASRec state with a gated mean of the last five valid prefix embeddings, then predict with shared item embeddings. Train with the target plus 64 uniform negatives, excluding padding and the user's observed training items.

Evaluate baseline, A, B and C using unchanged four-category macro validation HR@10. Track per-category margins and GPU seconds; stop within the shared budget. Recent pooling addresses stale influence; context-conditioned discrimination targets broadly plausible associations. Neither mechanism establishes compatibility or fulfilled demand.
INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 2: The recommended first experiment is **convex recent-context gated SASRec with uniform multi-negative candidate cross-entropy**. It targets stale historical influence and broadly plausible but weak immediate matches, while testing the observed ranking-separation problem. Improvements remain unverified.

### Insights and directions

**Starting-point insights.** (1) SASRec already learns selective historical attention, including recency. Explicit recent pooling therefore needs a controlled comparison. (2) Users report historical interests overwhelming current purpose. Convex fusion can attenuate the historical branch; the previous additive residual cannot explicitly do so. (3) Electronics has 14.95% sampled wrong ranks and a −0.4812 tenth-percentile margin. These motivate stronger discrimination, but do not establish validation HR@10 gains or severe embedding collapse.

**Related-work insights.** (1) MSMT-LSI and Mixture of Sequence motivate temporal fusion, without establishing this variant’s effectiveness. (2) gSASRec uses generalized BCE; eSASRec combines sampled softmax with architectural changes. Neither isolates our proposed loss. (3) Enhanced BPR motivates avoiding aggressive hard mining. Observed-positive exclusion still leaves unobserved relevant negatives.

### Framework

| Direction | Existing mechanism | Proposed minimal test | Remaining gap |
|---|---|---|---|
| Temporal relevance | Multiscale encoders | Normalized convex recent-context gate | Recency is not verified intent |
| Ranking discrimination | Multi-negative losses | Uniform candidate cross-entropy | Restricted sampling and false negatives |
| Functional relevance | Transition/text branches | Defer initially | Transitions do not verify compatibility |

### Candidate assessment

Scores are originality / potential / user alignment / model alignment / implementation difficulty; higher difficulty means harder. Each entry assesses every dimension positively and negatively.

| Idea | Scores | Positive / negative assessments |
|---|---|---|
| A: additive recent gate | 3/5/6/4/2 | O: targeted / familiar; P: extensible / retains historical coefficient; U: adds recent evidence / cannot attenuate history; M: context test / uncertain separation; D: small / masking needed |
| B: multi-negative CE | 2/7/4/8/3 | O: targeted / established; P: reusable / sampling sensitive; U: discrimination / no temporal mechanism; M: targets wrong ranks / transfer uncertain; D: batched / memory cost |
| C: A+B | 3/6/7/8/4 | O: combined / incremental; P: ablatable / confounded without controls; U: recent evidence / stale branch persists; M: discrimination / false negatives; D: modest / predictor parity |
| **D: convex gate+B** | **3/7/8/8/5** | O: coherent revision / established components; P: interpretable / limited novelty; U: attenuates stale context and sharpens matches / cannot verify prerequisites; M: targets wrong ranks / gains unverified; D: feasible / sampling and controls require care |

### Selected experiment

At 0% progress, choose D. Fuse consistently normalized SASRec and causal five-item pooled features using `(1−g)h+gr`; initialize gate weights to zero and bias to −2. Train with 64 uniform negatives and temperature 1. This is candidate-set CE, not corrected full-catalog softmax. Preserve duplicate samples.

Compare baseline, gate-only, loss-only and combined models under identical checkpoint rules; fixed convex and additive controls follow only if budget remains. Track branch norms, gates, per-category margins and GPU seconds. Select by pinned four-category macro validation HR@10. Paired stratified bootstrap intervals are exploratory and do not remove selection optimism. Sampled HR is not full-catalog HR.

Recent fusion addresses stale influence; context-conditioned discrimination targets broadly plausible associations. Neither establishes compatibility or fulfilled demand.
