{
  "description": "Augment each category’s SASRec scores with a popularity-centered, shrunk last-item transition residual. Retain unchanged SASRec through zero correction weight and explicitly test whether improvements arise from transition information or popularity suppression.",
  "motivation": "The supplied qualitative diagnosis suggests broad associations can dilute specific recent matches, but does not prove that greater recency emphasis helps. Transition counts provide an inexpensive complementary signal. Algebra exposes a shortcut: for unobserved pairs, the residual penalizes destination popularity in proportion to source support. Controlled comparisons are therefore necessary before attributing gains to specific transitions.",
  "implementation_notes": "Use only the prescribed training histories, retaining their existing chronological order. Count every adjacent nonpadding pair within each user's training history, including repeated transitions; never cross user or category boundaries. Let n_i=sum_j C[i,j]. Define destination popularity over all training interaction occurrences, p[j]=(f[j]+1)/(sum_k f[k]+M), for the M eligible nonpadding items. Fix tau=20 for this initial experiment as an explicit heuristic, not an established optimum. Set r(i,j)=(C[i,j]-n_i*p[j])/(n_i+tau); unseen source rows produce exactly zero correction.\n\nTrain the unchanged per-category SASRec with preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, and the environment's SASREC_EPOCHS default of 200. Obtain normalization statistics once per category from 10,000 training prefix–candidate pairs sampled with replacement using a separate seed-42 generator: sample uniformly from valid next-item training positions, use their causal prefixes truncated exactly as during training, and draw a uniform nonpadding candidate excluding the prefix and its next target. Skip positions without an eligible candidate and record the resulting count. Compute neural-score population standard deviation and residual root mean square in float64; clamp both at epsilon=1e-8 and serialize them. If the sample is empty, use scales of one and record the missing evidence. Do not normalize over evaluation candidates. Use s/sigma_s + alpha*r/RMS_r; dividing baseline scores by a positive category constant preserves its ordering at alpha=0.\n\nSelect a single shared alpha across all four categories from {0,0.25,0.5,0.75,1}, using only pinned macro validation HR@10. Break equal aggregate hit counts by choosing smaller alpha. Preserve the evaluator's tie handling and candidate sets. Save category scores and each user's binary hit for every configuration. Avoid independently selecting category weights: five choices per category would permit 625 combinations. Keep a cumulative configuration ledger across subsequent searches; small searches can still overfit validation.\n\nFor exploratory attribution, compare corrections -p[j], -log(p[j]), exact penalty -n_i*p[j]/(n_i+tau), count-only C[i,j]/(n_i+tau), and log-lift log((C[i,j]+tau*p[j])/((n_i+tau)*p[j])). Normalize each correction by its training-sample RMS and independently tune its strength on the same five-value grid. Unobserved log-lift pairs have a destination-independent correction; this avoids the residual's specific popularity penalty but can emphasize rare observed destinations. These controls are separate exploratory analyses, not free additional evidence for the primary selected model. Before claiming transition-specific gains, compare a randomized transition multigraph obtained through endpoint swaps that preserve source and destination totals; record seed, requested swaps, and accepted swaps. A history-aware extension averages corrections over the last five nonpadding items with normalized weights proportional to 0.5^age, age zero being newest. Treat this fixed extension as an additional exploratory configuration, not an established optimum. All computation and failed trials count against the shared budget; prioritize the primary branch and popularity controls before extensions.\n\nReport target-pair support and candidate-pair support separately: for each validation user, target support indicates C[last,target]>0, and candidate support is the fraction of the fixed 100 candidates with positive counts. Report HR@10 changes by transition support, training source-frequency quartiles, target-popularity quartiles, and history-length quartiles, with category-specific bin boundaries derived only from training data, retained duplicate boundaries, and subgroup sizes. Do not treat these strata as additional selection objectives.\n\nStore counts as sorted CSR rows; avoid an M-by-M dense matrix. Candidate scoring gathers sparse counts. Full-catalog scoring uses chunked neural matrix multiplication, initializes the analytic negative-popularity term, and adds sparse observed-edge corrections. Empty histories bypass potentially undefined all-padding neural features and return training popularity; a nonempty history with an unseen transition source receives the neural score alone. Apply the benchmark's existing seen-item exclusions without modifying evaluation.\n\nProvide a shared scorer score(features, items, source_items) for inference and architecture-aware probes. Preserve predict_score(features, items) as the compatible neural-component scorer and explicitly identify its scope in metric definitions; probes measuring deployed scores must call the shared scorer with source IDs. At diagnostic position t, use sequence[:,t] as source for the causal feature predicting positive[:,t]. Mask padding targets and sources, handle B-by-L tensors explicitly, and never infer source IDs from cached features. Preserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). view.model remains the actual trained nn.Module. Run under no_grad, restore the prior training mode in finally, and return metrics, metric_definitions, observation counts, and nulls for unavailable measurements. Distinguish scaled margins from rank-based evidence.\n\nKeep controlled temporal interventions supplementary: remove matched newest/oldest counts, shuffle earlier items while retaining the last item, and shuffle the entire history using fixed repeated permutations and unchanged validation candidates. Report paired hit, target-rank, and score changes with explicit tie handling. Frequency-matched replacements can help assess popularity confounding. These are inference-reliance probes; they do not establish causal intent or retrained ablation performance.\n\nFor uncertainty, run 10,000 seed-42 paired bootstrap replicates, independently resampling validation users within each category while using identical indices for compared models and averaging the four category differences equally. Keep each user's candidate set intact. Label the observed difference and percentile interval exploratory: the interval is conditional on trained models and candidates and does not adjust for winner selection, training seeds, or negative-sampling variability. Do not create new splits or access held-out test outcomes during search.\n\nPreserve DATA_DIR=os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], all four categories, dataset routing, single-GPU execution, and artifacts beneath the trial directory. Update the predictor writer to serialize/load CSR counts, popularity, tau, scales, correction type, shared alpha, routing, and fallback rules. Check loaded-versus-training scorer parity on fixed training histories and candidates, including sparse rows, empty histories, padding, and candidate batches. Available metadata consists only of public titles, descriptions, and timestamps; do not consume the generic input's unavailable ratings, reviews, categories, prices, or stores. For the twenty training users, catalog coverage, popularity, concentration, and evidence-qualified simulator judgments remain supplementary and cannot establish relevance or latent diversity/conformity. No benchmark gain has been measured.",
  "pseudocode": "for category in four_categories:\n    fit unchanged SASRec using prescribed split, seeds, and epoch environment\n    C = CSR adjacent-transition counts from nonpadding training histories\n    n = row_sums(C)\n    p = add_one_smoothed_training_occurrence_popularity()\n    tau = 20\n    residual(i,j) = (C[i,j] - n[i]*p[j]) / (n[i] + tau)\n    training_pairs = sample_training_prefix_candidates(seed=42, count=10000)\n    sigma_s = max(population_std(neural_scores(training_pairs)), 1e-8)\n    rms_r = max(root_mean_square(residual_scores(training_pairs)), 1e-8)\n    save counts, p, tau, scales, routing, fallback rules\nfor alpha in [0, .25, .5, .75, 1]:\n    score(h,j) = neural(h,j)/sigma_s + alpha*residual(last_nonpadding(h),j)/rms_r\n    empty_history_score(j) = p[j]\n    obtain pinned validation hits without changing candidates or tie handling\n    save every user's hit and category score\nselect shared alpha by four-category macro HR@10; prefer smaller alpha on ties\nwrite predictor using the same scorer and serialized artifacts\nverify scorer parity on fixed training-only examples\nrun prespecified supplementary controls, support strata, and temporal probes within budget\nreport paired stratified bootstrap interval as descriptive and selection-unadjusted\nrestore model mode after every diagnostic probe",
