INFO:self_evolverec:The new idea for model in iteration 2:
{
  "description": "Add a zero-initialized scalar-gated recent-history residual to SASRec and train it with uniform multiple-negative sampled-candidate cross-entropy. At each valid position, add a projected, branch-normalized, exponentially weighted mean of up to five recent input-item embeddings to the original sequence feature. Compare baseline, residual-only, loss-only, and combined variants.",
  "motivation": "The recent branch explicitly targets two reported user failures: historical interests overpowering current purpose, and broadly plausible associations displacing specific recent matches. Multiple-negative competition targets measured weak ranking separation, particularly Electronics’ 14.95% sampled wrong-rank rate. These are mechanism hypotheses rather than demonstrated improvements. Neither mechanism establishes equipment compatibility, complementary function, whether a purchase satisfied a need, or unobserved simulator traits. The embedding similarity measurements do not justify treating representation collapse as the primary problem.",
  "implementation_notes": "Keep DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, all four dataset routes, original splits, and SASREC_EPOCHS default 200. Write all artifacts beneath the trial directory and execute on one GPU. Do not access hidden test files or unavailable ratings, reviews, categories, prices, or stores.\n\nArchitecture: preserve the baseline SASRec feature path, including its existing embedding scaling, normalization, causal mask, and padding behavior. For the recent branch, use raw shared item embeddings and m_t = sum_{j=0..4}(0.7^j * valid_{t-j} * e_{t-j}) / max(sum_{j=0..4}(0.7^j * valid_{t-j}), epsilon). Use explicit left padding or causal slicing, never wrapping tensor shifts. Include only input positions <= t, never the next-item target. Return zero for an empty window. Apply LayerNorm only to this branch, then a learned linear projection P. Define fused features as h_t + alpha * P(LN(m_t)), with alpha initialized to exactly zero and P initialized with ordinary nonzero weights. Initialize projection bias to zero; remask padding after normalization, projection, and fusion. Do not zero both alpha and P, which would block initial learning. The original negative-biased sigmoid convex gate only approximately preserved predictions and had uncontrolled branch scale; this residual replaces it. Shared dropout behavior still makes training predictions stochastic, but the residual contributes exactly zero at initialization for the same baseline forward pass. Keep temperature fixed at 1 and use unchanged shared item-dot-product scoring.\n\nObjective: at every valid next-item position, sample k=32 negatives independently with replacement from real item IDs outside the user's complete training history and current target. Exclude padding and skip positions with no eligible negative. Training-history exclusion must not consult validation/test interactions. Repeated negative IDs remain repeated denominator terms; repeated targets remain separate sequence-position examples. The positive appears exactly once in column zero. Use cross-entropy over these 33 logits without logQ correction and describe it as a sampled-candidate ranking surrogate, not calibrated full-catalog sampled softmax or gBCE. The proposal is user-dependent, so identical uniform correction terms cannot simply be assumed to cancel against the deterministically inserted positive. Unobserved positives remain possible false negatives. Avoid in-batch candidate sharing in this initial experiment. Use bounded rejection sampling with an exact eligible-set fallback to avoid hangs for dense users.\n\nReduction and optimization: average loss over all eligible positions in a batch, not over chunk means. Backpropagate chunk loss sums divided by the batch's eligible-position count, retaining the shared feature graph until the final chunk when necessary; take one optimizer step per original batch. Start with the baseline optimizer, learning rate, batch size, and other hyperparameters unchanged. Record their resolved values, model initialization, random-generator states, negative-sampling configuration, and data fingerprints. Chunking must change memory use only, not the objective. The architecture is small, but rejection sampling, chunked gradients, and four-category predictor integration justify increasing code difficulty from 4 to 5.\n\nIntegration: save window, decay, LayerNorm, projection, scalar gate, and every baseline inference parameter in each category checkpoint. Update the predictor writer so working/predict.py reconstructs the trained module and preserves dataset routing. Require reload parity for identical sequences and candidate IDs, padding-zero checks, and causal-prefix invariance before expensive training. Preserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). Keep view.model as the actual nn.Module, view.item_emb as its shared embedding, view.log2feats as fused feature computation, and view.predict_score identical to inference scoring. Preserve the (None, sequence, positive, negative) B x L loader contract. Return measured metrics and metric_definitions, and generate the interpretation prompt from both. Continue embedding-similarity and margin measurements with matched probe samples; mark category breakdowns unavailable because item categories are unavailable. Report diagnostic sample counts and avoid interpreting training margins as validation HR@10.\n\nEvaluation: predeclare four variants: baseline with original loss; A with residual and original loss; B with original architecture and new loss; C with both. Keep initialization seeds, data order, pinned evaluation users/candidates, and evaluation schedules matched wherever feasible. Record separate RNG streams for model initialization, training sampling, and diagnostics so probes do not perturb training. Use the host's pinned 200 users/category, 99 unique negatives, and evaluation seed 42. Do not replace or modify its evaluator. Use common macro validation HR@10 for deployment selection. Use a predeclared final checkpoint for controlled ablation comparisons; report best-checkpoint results as exploratory and record every checkpoint opportunity. A cheap h'=h+alpha*LN(e_t) last-item residual, alpha=0, can isolate immediate-item signal in a subsequent explicitly budgeted experiment; it is not silently added to the initial four variants.\n\nIf the authorized evaluator exposes per-user validation hits, save binary hit@10 with user/category IDs, use 10,000 paired bootstrap replicates resampling 200 users within each category, and report macro differences with 95% intervals plus category differences and discordant-user counts. Predeclare the three baseline comparisons and use Holm-adjusted paired-test p-values, with exact paired discordance tests when their exchangeability assumptions are appropriate. Ordinary intervals remain pointwise unless simultaneous intervals are constructed. These analyses describe user-sampling uncertainty conditional on trained models and fixed candidates; they exclude training-seed and candidate-sampling uncertainty and do not correct checkpoint or variant selection. If per-user hits are not exposed, report available host metrics and explicitly omit unavailable intervals rather than changing evaluation.\n\nCompute and risks: allocate at most 10,000 GPU-seconds per variant, with a shared 3,200-second reserve for diagnostics, debugging, and failures; total charged work must never exceed 43,200 GPU-seconds. Unused allocations need not be spent. Retain the 200-epoch default, subject to each predeclared resource cap, and label capped runs as resource-limited. Compare matched training resources where caps prevent common epoch completion; do not present them as equal-epoch ablations. Keep equal checkpoint opportunities and stop by budget rather than statistical significance. Repeated validation selection can overfit 800 fixed validation users. Item popularity and broad lexical or collaborative associations may remain shortcuts; the recency branch itself does not enforce specific purpose. Recent purchases may also be transient noise or satisfied demand. Track the supplied user-facing rejection reasons only when actually observed; retain uncertain judgments separately. Future potential is reduced from 8 to 7 because the contribution is incremental and benefits remain unverified. No gains, significance, compatibility understanding, or held-out generalization are claimed before measurement.",
  "pseudocode": "class RecentResidualSASRec(SASRec):\n    def __init__(self, ...):\n        super().__init__(...)\n        self.recent_norm = LayerNorm(hidden_dim)\n        self.recent_projection = Linear(hidden_dim, hidden_dim)\n        initialize_nonzero_weights_and_zero_bias(self.recent_projection)\n        self.alpha = Parameter(0.0)\n\n    def log2feats(self, sequence):\n        h = super().log2feats(sequence)  # Preserve baseline path exactly.\n        valid = sequence != 0\n        e = self.item_emb(sequence)     # Raw shared input embeddings.\n        numerator = zeros_like(e)\n        denominator = zeros_like(valid, dtype=float)\n        for lag in range(5):\n            # Left shifts here mean explicit left padding, never cyclic roll.\n            prior_e = causal_left_pad_shift(e, lag)\n            prior_valid = causal_left_pad_shift(valid, lag)\n            weight = (0.7 ** lag) * prior_valid\n            numerator += weight[..., None] * prior_e\n            denominator += weight\n        m = numerator / clamp_min(denominator[..., None], epsilon)\n        m = zero_where_empty(m, denominator)\n        r = self.recent_norm(m)\n        r = mask_padding(r, valid)\n        r = self.recent_projection(r)\n        r = mask_padding(r, valid)\n        return mask_padding(h + self.alpha * r, valid)\n\nfor batch in training_batches:\n    _, sequence, positive, _ = batch\n    # Dataset/collator retains the aligned users' training-only exclusion sets.\n    positions = eligible_positions(sequence, positive, batch_training_histories)\n    if len(positions) == 0:\n        continue\n    optimizer.zero_grad()\n    features = model.log2feats(sequence)\n    for chunk_index, chunk in enumerate(chunks(positions)):\n        negatives = uniform_sample_with_replacement(\n            k=32,\n            exclude=padding_and_current_target_and_user_training_history,\n            bounded_rejection_with_exact_fallback=True)\n        candidates = concatenate(chunk.positive[:, None], negatives, axis=1)\n        logits = item_dot_product(features[chunk.positions], candidates)\n        loss = cross_entropy(logits, zero_target_column, reduction='sum')\n        loss = loss / len(positions)\n        loss.backward(retain_graph=not_last_chunk(chunk_index))\n    optimizer.step()\n\n# Predeclare baseline, A=residual, B=loss, C=both.\n# Keep training configuration and evaluation opportunities matched.\n# Fixed-final checkpoints support ablations; best validation checkpoints are exploratory.\n# Select deployment using unchanged host macro validation HR@10.\n# Save all architecture parameters and emit the matching routed predictor.\n# Preserve diagnostic adapters; count all charged work against the shared budget.",
  "originality": {
    "score": 4,
    "positive": "Combines causal recent-interest adaptation, exact residual initialization, and stronger sampled competition in direct response to both supplied diagnoses.",
    "negative": "Short/long preference fusion, zero-initialized residual gates, and multiple-negative objectives are established; their combination provides limited methodological novelty."
  },
  "future_potential": {
