INFO:self_evolverec:The new idea for diagnosis in iteration 2:
{
  "description": "Add a learned scalar gate to each per-dataset SASRec model, blending every causal sequence state with a normalized mean of its five most recent valid item embeddings. Retain contextual-state pooling as a controlled alternative rather than the selected design. Pair the experiment with architecture-aware history-perturbation diagnostics.",
  "motivation": "The supplied feedback identifies insufficient emphasis on repeated recent interests. Pooling item embeddings introduces an explicit recent-interest component without averaging contextual states trained for different next-item targets. HAM and HGN motivate this component, but do not establish gains on this benchmark. At 0% progress, this small intervention is preferable to expensive negative mining or a larger architectural replacement.",
  "implementation_notes": "Preserve DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], all four dataset routes, preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, unchanged input splits and baseline loss/optimizer settings, and SASREC_EPOCHS with environment default 200. Keep execution single-GPU and artifacts beneath the trial directory. No external datasets, pretrained weights, hidden test files, or evaluator changes are permitted. The selected change requires a distinct matched training run; code below specifies its readout, not a replacement for the unavailable baseline training script.\n\nCompute causal pooling at every valid training position from the same item embedding table used by SASRec. Average the last five valid events including the current item; repeated events retain their multiplicity. Apply parameter-free LayerNorm separately to the contextual state and recent-item mean for the additional branch, then use z = h + sigmoid(gate_logit) * (LN(recent_mean) - LN(h)). This residual formulation preserves the original contextual feature at gate zero while controlling the added branch's scale. Initialize gate_logit to -4, giving an approximately 0.018 contribution; this approximates baseline initialization but is not exactly identical. Keep the SASRec output scoring function unchanged. Do not add item bias, loss changes, or sampling changes in this first comparison. Record the learned gate and feature norms separately by dataset; interpret gate magnitude cautiously because correlated branches can make it weakly identifiable.\n\nUse the final valid feature for inference, explicitly handling left or right padding and rejecting all-padding histories according to the baseline's existing policy. Save architecture name, window size, normalization epsilon, gate parameters, item mapping, and all baseline model configuration in checkpoints. Update the predictor writer so generated working/predict.py reconstructs the same readout, loads its state strictly, retains routing, and reproduces scores in evaluation mode. Match existing weight tying, padding embedding treatment, and item indexing.\n\nPreserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). view.model must remain the actual trained nn.Module, and view.item_emb, log2feats, predict_score, user_train, meta_data, and review_data must remain available. log2feats must return the blended features used for scoring. The loader remains (None, sequence, positive, negative), with B x L tensors. The prompt must include measured metrics, definitions, sample counts, missingness, and limitations. Preserve embedding alignment and positive-negative margin summaries; label them as training-pair diagnostics. Return null and count zero when measurements are undefined, rather than healthy-looking zeros. Keep unavailable category breakdowns empty. Only histories, timestamps, titles, and descriptions are available; do not use review ratings/text, item ratings/categories/prices/stores, or infer simulator diversity/conformity traits.\n\nFor training-prefix sensitivity, preselect up to 20 users per dataset with at least ten valid prefix events and a public next-item target; report actual coverage. These users are a separate diagnostic sample from the native twenty-user panel. Fix the target before perturbation and freeze the original truncated visible prefix so deletion cannot expose older items. Use a local seed-42 generator and cache a target plus up to 99 unique negatives excluding the user's public training-history items; report candidate shortages. These candidates are diagnostic only and do not replace official evaluator candidates. Evaluate paired original and perturbed prefixes without dropout or gradients. Delete two recent versus two immediately older events; add count-matched random controls and random deletions matched to each window's recency band. Keep the last interaction intact in the primary experiment. Analyze final-interaction deletion separately with an explicit last-surviving-state readout rule.\n\nCompare compact deletion with surviving absolute position IDs preserved, using an optional diagnostic position-ID hook without changing normal inference. Also test content replacement at fixed positions and sequence length, and position-ID shifts with identical content where valid embedding indices permit. Padding replacement changes attention masks and must be labeled as a separate perturbation. Optional backfilling is another separate experiment. Report paired observed-target score, positive-negative margin, target-rank, diagnostic HR@10, and top-10 overlap changes. Distinguish observed next-item targets from original-model top recommendations. Stratify by history length and target popularity computed only from training interactions. Report uncertainty by resampling users, not individual positions. Restore the prior model mode after probing. These measurements describe fitted-model dependence on training prefixes, may involve out-of-distribution inputs, and establish neither generalization nor causal user preferences. Retraining after deletion answers a different question.\n\nBefore the first comparison, declare two primary variants: unchanged SASRec and the proposed readout. Evaluate every ten epochs and at the final epoch, stop after five scheduled evaluations without improvement, and retain the earliest checkpoint on exact macro-score ties. Apply the same policy to both variants using the established pinned validation implementation; if that implementation is unavailable within training, leave official scoring and selection to the host rather than reconstructing it. Log every attempted variant and checkpoint comparison, including failures. The shared 43,200 GPU-second cap overrides planned runs; training, diagnosis, and failed trials all count. Further ablations require a recorded budget allocation before observing their results: final state, last-five contextual mean, last-five raw-embedding mean, fixed versus learned mixture, and equally sized older-item windows. Match normalization controls and comparable compute to distinguish recency from normalization or smoothing effects. Supplement cosine alignment with effective rank of centered valid sequence features, defined as exp(-sum(p_j log p_j)) for normalized nonzero singular values, and report sample size. Such controls are hypotheses to test, not completed results.\n\nSelect only by the unchanged host macro validation HR@10: 200 users per category, target plus 99 unique negatives, evaluation seed 42, equal category weights. Report every category and the macro score without cherry-picking. One changed hit changes category HR by 0.005 and macro HR by 0.00125. If per-user hit vectors are available from the established evaluation path, use 2,000 paired bootstrap replicates with local seed 42, resampling the same 200 user indices for baseline and variant independently within each category, then averaging the four differences with weight 1/4. Report percentile intervals and category differences. These intervals condition on trained models and fixed candidates; they exclude seed and candidate variability and do not correct adaptive model selection. Ordinary confidence intervals cannot establish that a selected tiny gain is robust. Freeze the winner before independent host test evaluation.\n\nRetain twenty real training users, five per category, for native full-catalog unseen-item recommendations. Record evidence excerpts, timestamps, feedback provenance, confidence, abstentions, and assessed coverage if panel judgments are available. No panel counts or explicit rejection labels have been supplied. Missing specifications are not compatibility conflicts; interactions do not establish ownership or satisfaction. Keep uncertain complements and possible redundancy out of negative labels. Panel feedback and optional budgeted full-catalog audits remain supplementary and never replace benchmark selection.",
  "pseudocode": "class RecentItemReadout(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, encoder, window=5, eps=1e-5):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.encoder = encoder\n        self.window = window\n        self.eps = eps\n        self.gate_logit = nn.Parameter(torch.tensor(-4.0))\n\n    @property\n    def item_emb(self):\n        return self.encoder.item_emb\n\n    def log2feats(self, sequence, position_ids=None):\n        # Add an optional position-ID hook to the existing encoder for probes.\n        # Ordinary training/inference keeps its original positional convention.\n        h = self.encoder.log2feats(sequence, position_ids=position_ids)\n        valid = sequence.ne(0)\n        embeddings = self.item_emb(sequence)\n        pooled_rows = []\n        for b in range(sequence.shape[0]):\n            positions = valid[b].nonzero(as_tuple=True)[0]\n            events = embeddings[b, positions]\n            # Event-order rolling means handle either padding convention.\n            cumulative = torch.cat([events.new_zeros(1, events.shape[-1]),\n                                    events.cumsum(dim=0)], dim=0)\n            end = torch.arange(1, len(positions)+1, device=sequence.device)\n            start = (end - self.window).clamp_min(0)\n            means = (cumulative[end] - cumulative[start]) / (end-start).unsqueeze(-1)\n            row = torch.zeros_like(h[b]).index_copy(0, positions, means)\n            pooled_rows.append(row)\n        recent = torch.stack(pooled_rows)\n        normalized_h = F.layer_norm(h, (h.shape[-1],), eps=self.eps)\n        normalized_recent = F.layer_norm(recent, (h.shape[-1],), eps=self.eps)\n        gate = torch.sigmoid(self.gate_logit)\n        z = h + gate * (normalized_recent - normalized_h)\n        return z * valid.unsqueeze(-1)\n\n    def predict_score(self, features, items):\n        return self.encoder.predict_score(features, items)\n\n# Vectorize across rows for production once padding conventions are confirmed.\n# Train using the unchanged baseline loss on valid positive positions.\n# Use the largest valid sequence index for inference; handle empty rows explicitly.\n# Save encoder configuration, architecture, window, eps, and complete state_dict.\n# Predictor writer reconstructs exactly this module and loads strictly.\n# Diagnosis runs the actual scoring path, restoring the previous model mode.\n# run_full_diagnosis returns {'metrics': values, 'metric_definitions': definitions}.\n# diagnosis_interpreter_prompt includes measured values, definitions, and limitations.\n# Perturbation probes freeze windows/targets/candidates and record positional controls.\n# Official selection uses only pinned four-category macro validation HR@10.",
  "originality": {
    "score": 3,
    "positive": "Pairs a narrowly targeted recent-item residual with architecture-aware controls that distinguish recency, positional effects, and smoothing.",
