INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 1: At 10% progress, prioritize a small, controlled extension: **recent-conditioned SASRec with 32-negative cross-entropy**. Its benefits remain hypotheses; the supplied 0.5050 score describes the earlier baseline.

**Insights from the starting point**

1. Users report stale interests crowding out current purposes. A recent-history residual offers a direct intervention while preserving learned long-term preferences.
2. Broad similarity produces weak task or content connections. Learning directed recent-to-next-item associations could sharpen these connections, although histories cannot establish equipment compatibility.
3. Electronics has an 8.42% training negative-win rate and 0.210 tenth-percentile margin. These motivate stronger candidate discrimination, but neither establishes the cause of validation errors.

**Insights from related work**

1. Sampled softmax emphasizes high-scoring negatives [Wu et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.02327). Thirty-two uniform alternatives provide a simple candidate-competition experiment without dynamic mining.
2. gSASRec addresses sampling-induced overconfidence through generalized BCE [gSASRec](https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.07192). It supplies an objective comparator rather than evidence that CE will win.
3. ICSRec learns intents from subsequences without intent labels [ICSRec](https://doi.org/10.1145/3616855.3635773). This supports using observable recent prefixes, while leaving explicit intent boundaries unverified.

**Framework and candidate assessment**

Organize experiments along three directions: temporal representation, candidate discrimination, and candidate-specific evidence. Each assessment below gives a score followed by a positive/negative reason; O=originality, P=potential, U=user alignment, M=model alignment, D=implementation difficulty, where higher D means harder.

| Idea | O | P | U | M | D |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 32-negative CE only | 2: established/simple | 6: scalable/limited novelty | 4: sharper competition/no recency | 8: targets separation/cause uncertain | 3: local change/masking required |
| **Gated recent residual + CE** | 4: useful combination/familiar components | 8: extensible/extra tuning | 8: recency and specific transitions/no compatibility labels | 8: competing negatives/no guaranteed HR gain | 4: compact branch/predictor coordination |
| Candidate-conditioned attention, inspired by [DIN](https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.06978) | 4: targeted adaptation/established attention | 7: candidate evidence/catalog cost | 8: specific connections/redundancy unresolved | 6: discrimination hypothesis/indirect evidence | 7: expressive/chunked scoring |

**Selected algorithm**

For every causal prefix, pool its last five nonpadding item embeddings with exponential recency weights. Project this summary through a separate matrix and add it to SASRec features through a prefix-dependent sigmoid gate and a zero-initialized scalar. Separate projection and target embeddings allow asymmetric transition associations.

Train masked next-item CE against 32 unique uniform negatives, excluding the target and known training-history positives. This targets outdated-interest dominance and loose historical connections; it does not infer compatibility or treat uncertain simulator judgments as dislikes.

Compare baseline, CE-only, residual-only, and combined variants within the shared budget. Select exclusively by pinned four-category macro validation HR@10. Preserve seeds, splits, routing, environment paths, and the 200-epoch environment default. Update predictor generation and diagnostics together; report measured deployed margins, counts, and exclusions without equating training separation or embedding alignment with recommendation quality.
INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 2: At 10% progress, prioritize **recent-conditioned SASRec with 32-negative candidate cross-entropy** as a controlled extension. Benefits remain hypotheses; 0.5050 describes the supplied earlier baseline.

**Insights from the starting point**

1. Users report stale interests displacing current purposes. A causal recent-history residual directly targets this pattern while preserving long-term features.
2. Broad similarity produces weak task/content connections. Directed recent-to-target learning may sharpen connections, but cannot establish compatibility or immediate demand.
3. Electronics has an 8.42% training negative-win rate and 0.210 tenth-percentile margin. These motivate candidate-discrimination experiments without identifying the cause of validation errors.

**Insights from related work**

1. [HGN](https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.09217), [FPMC](https://www.ra.ethz.ch/cdstore/www2010/www/p811.pdf), and [SHAN](https://ijcai.org/proceedings/2018/546) already combine recent and persistent preferences. Novelty is a compact adaptation, not gated recency or directionality generally.
2. [Sampled-softmax analysis](https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.02327) motivates competing negatives; [gSASRec](https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.07192) provides a calibration comparator. Candidate CE is not full-catalog likelihood, and unobserved negatives may be relevant.
3. [ICSRec](https://doi.org/10.1145/3616855.3635773) supports subsequence-derived intent; [ReZero](https://proceedings.mlr.press/v161/bachlechner21a/bachlechner21a.pdf) supports initially inactive residuals. Neither establishes this proposal’s ranking benefit.

**Framework and candidate assessment**

Directions are temporal representation, candidate discrimination, and candidate-specific evidence. Scores include positive/negative reasons; O=originality, P=potential, U=user alignment, M=model alignment, D=difficulty; higher D means harder.

| Idea | O | P | U | M | D |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 32-negative CE only | 2: established/simple | 6: scalable/limited novelty | 4: competition/no recency | 8: separation/cause uncertain | 3: local change/exclusions |
| **Gated recent residual + CE** | 3: compact adaptation/strong precedent | 7: extensible/extra tuning | 8: recency and transitions/no compatibility labels | 8: candidate competition/unverified HR gain | 5: small branch/coordinated deployment |
| [DIN](https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.06978)-inspired candidate attention | 4: targeted adaptation/established mechanism | 7: specific evidence/catalog cost | 8: connections/redundancy unresolved | 6: discrimination/indirect evidence | 7: expressive/chunked scoring |

**Selected algorithm**

Pool the last five causal nonpadding embeddings with exponential weights. Add a projected summary through a prefix-dependent scalar sigmoid gate and zero-initialized residual scalar. Separate projection permits asymmetric associations; fixed decay does not learn relevance. Initially only the scalar receives branch gradients; initialize the projection normally and monitor activation.

Train CE over one target and 32 unique uniform alternatives, excluding padding, target, and complete known training positives inside the user-aware sampler. Preserve tensor-only batches. Fix temperature to one; uncertain simulator judgments never become dislikes.

Predeclare baseline, CE-only, residual-only, and combined variants, each capped at 10,800 charged GPU-seconds. Preserve seeds, splits, routing, paths, and the 200-epoch default. Use identical validation cadence and earliest-checkpoint tie-breaking; select by pinned macro HR@10. Save paired user hits and report stratified bootstrap intervals with their conditional limitations. Update predictor and measured diagnostics together; deployed margins, counts, and exclusions must be explicit. Additional pooling, gate, and gBCE controls require remaining budget.
