      - Analysis: {"status":"NEEDS_IMPROVEMENT","core_findings":["Electronics has the weakest fitted separation: 21.89% of positive–negative pairs are misranked, and recent-event deletion produces no mean HR@10 change. Movies and Industrial show stronger fitted separation and larger recent-deletion effects.","CDs has nearly perfect fitted pair separation, while embedding alignment is greatest in CDs and Industrial. These training-only statistics establish neither representation collapse nor held-out recommendation quality."],"key_implications":["Electronics rankings are relatively insensitive to the measured history interventions, whereas Movies rankings depend more strongly on recent events and earlier-event order.","Large differences in fitted margins indicate uneven training separation across datasets; margin magnitudes also depend on logit scale.","Target contamination in the reliance probes prevents interpreting their results as validation gains or causal reliance."],"evidence":{"headline_metrics":{"Electronics:pos_neg_neg_beats_pos_rate":0.2189,"Electronics:delete_recent_hr10_delta":0.0,"Movies_and_TV:delete_recent_hr10_delta":-0.155,"CDs_and_Vinyl:pos_neg_neg_beats_pos_rate":0.0008},"brief_metric_read":{"Electronics:pos_neg_neg_beats_pos_rate":"Highest fitted pair misranking rate.","Electronics:delete_recent_hr10_delta":"95% interval [-0.02, 0.02]; no clear mean effect.","Movies_and_TV:delete_recent_hr10_delta":"95% interval [-0.215, -0.10]; fitted ranking worsens.","CDs_and_Vinyl:pos_neg_neg_beats_pos_rate":"Almost all sampled fitted pairs are correctly ordered."}}}
    
