trols only through an architecture adapter that accepts position IDs and preserves the original computation on unchanged inputs. For a fixed survivor set, physically compact and right-align survivors while either retaining their original learned position IDs or assigning conventional compacted slot IDs. Preserve survivor order, latest/readout item, checkpoint, candidates, and evaluation mode. Full history versus survivors carrying original IDs measures removal at fixed survivor positions, conditional on the adapter; identical survivors with original versus compacted IDs measures positional reassignment conditional on removal. Deleting older items preserves the readout item, although its contextual representation may change. If a separate latest-item deletion is reported, explicitly state that the readout item changes too.\n\nPosition IDs select embeddings and do not define attention visibility. Inspect the actual architecture before implementing controls. A correctly masked branch must exclude padded keys/values at every layer and avoid fully blocked padded query rows or NaNs. In torch.nn.MultiheadAttention Boolean masks, True means blocked. Do not introduce zero-item interior holes as an assumed removal control. If adding validity masks changes an originally unmasked checkpoint's computation, establish its unchanged-history baseline separately and label results as belonging to that modified inference branch. Do not attribute original-to-masked differences to history removal. Where supported, compare correctly masked interior-hole inference against compacted survivors carrying original IDs; agreement is an implementation check conditional on equivalent attention/readout semantics. Use unchanged-input tolerances atol=1e-5 and rtol=1e-4 for float32 scores/features; report dtype and maximum deviations, and mark unsupported or failed controls unavailable rather than silently adjusting tolerances. Do not retrain solely to enable this optional control during the initial p