INFO:self_evolverec:Research reports (2 report(s)):
INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 1: Select a **candidate-conditioned recent-history residual** for the next experiment. At 10% progress, a small scoring change offers a clearer test than another temporal architecture.

**Insights from the starting point.** (1) Zero initialization preserves baseline scores initially. This makes an added branch easier to evaluate safely. (2) Recent pooling compresses several actions into one vector. Candidate-specific weighting could retain distinctions that shared pooling loses. (3) Prior residual variants scored 0.4575 and 0.4138 versus the supplied baseline’s 0.5050. These observations discourage repeating increasingly complex pooling, although they do not isolate why performance declined.

**Insights from related work.** (1) Multi-Level Interaction Reranking [1] motivates candidate–history interactions. Such interactions can test whether a candidate has specific historical support. (2) MiaSRec [2] challenges last-item dominance through multiple session representations. This favors retaining several recent items rather than replacing SASRec with last-item similarity. (3) gSASRec [3] motivates objective-only experiments with more negatives. Its calibration findings do not establish that a new scoring branch improves HR@10.

**Framework and directions.** Separate three axes: evidence selection, scoring, and optimization. Fixed recent pooling selects evidence before seeing the candidate; candidate attention selects evidence during scoring; multiple-negative objectives change competition without changing evidence. The next gap to test is candidate-specific evidence, with optimization held fixed.

**Ideas and assessments.** Scores are originality/future potential/user alignment/model alignment/difficulty; higher difficulty means harder. Each assessment gives a positive and negative reason.

| Idea | Scores | Assessment: positive / negative |
|---|---|---|
| Candidate-conditioned recent residual [1,2] | 4/7/7/6/4 | O: targeted combination / established attention; F: extensible / incremental; U: specific support / similarity can mislead; M: candidate separation / no proven margin benefit; D: small scorer / predictor integration |
| Scratch TF-IDF recent-content residual | 4/6/7/5/5 | O: permitted content fusion / familiar matching; F: interpretable / lexical sparsity; U: finer distinctions / wording misses intent; M: extra evidence / margins may worsen; D: lightweight features / vocabulary and mapping work |
| Uniform 32-negative objective only [3] | 2/6/4/8/3 | O: controlled test / established; F: reusable / limited novelty; U: stronger competition / no purpose signal; M: targets wrong ranks / false negatives; D: simple loss / sampling cost |

**Chosen mechanism.** Keep SASRec intact. Add a zero-scaled score residual using candidate attention over the latest five causal input embeddings, conditioned additionally on the SASRec state. This explicitly targets unsupported broad associations and missed entertainment mood/intensity distinctions; neither compatibility nor ownership is inferred.

The model-facing target is the observed 17.4–24.0% training wrong-rank rate, especially Industrial_and_Scientific. These are training diagnostics, not validation results. Compare baseline versus residual with unchanged loss, then consider objective changes separately. Select exclusively through pinned four-category macro validation HR@10. No improvement is claimed before measurement.
INFO:self_evolverec:  Report 2: Select a **candidate-conditioned recent-history residual** for the next experiment. At 10% progress, retain the stronger baseline and test this bounded scoring change before adding temporal states or changing objectives.

**Insights from the starting point.** (1) Zero initialization preserves baseline scores initially, enabling a controlled addition. It does not preserve subsequent optimization trajectories. (2) Recent pooling compresses several actions into one vector. Candidate-specific weighting may preserve distinctions lost by shared pooling. (3) Prior residual variants scored 0.4575 and 0.4138 versus the supplied baseline’s 0.5050. These observations discourage further pooling complexity, although unmatched experiments cannot isolate causes.

**Insights from related work.** (1) DIN directly establishes candidate-conditioned pooling; DIEN adds target-conditioned recurrent evolution. The proposed contribution is narrower: bounded-window, zero-initialized integration into SASRec. (2) MIR motivates richer candidate–history interactions, while MiaSRec motivates retaining multiple actions. Neither establishes benefits for this benchmark. (3) gSASRec supports separate objective experiments. Sampled-metric and model-selection research additionally require distinguishing benchmark gains from full-catalog quality and selection optimism.

**Framework and directions.** Separate evidence selection, scoring, and optimization. Fixed pooling selects evidence before seeing candidates; candidate attention selects during scoring; multiple-negative objectives change competition. A fourth axis—evaluation—separates training diagnostics, pinned sampled validation, and full-catalog simulator recommendations.

**Ideas and assessments.** Scores are originality/future potential/user alignment/model alignment/difficulty; higher difficulty means harder. Each assessment gives a positive and negative reason.

| Idea | Scores | Assessment: positive / negative |
|---|---|---|
| Candidate-conditioned recent residual [DIN, MIR, MiaSRec] | 3/6/7/6/6 | O: bounded residual integration / strong precedent; F: extensible / prior pooling regressions; U: specific evidence / similarity can mislead; M: candidate separation / unproven benefit; D: small branch / context and cache integration |
| Scratch TF-IDF recent-content residual | 4/6/7/5/5 | O: permitted fusion / familiar matching; F: interpretable / sparse vocabulary; U: finer distinctions / lexical shortcuts; M: extra evidence / possible margin degradation; D: lightweight features / mapping work |
| Uniform 32-negative objective only [gSASRec] | 2/6/4/8/3 | O: controlled comparison / established; F: reusable / limited novelty; U: stronger competition / no purpose signal; M: targets wrong ranks / false negatives; D: simple objective / sampling cost |

**Chosen mechanism.** Keep SASRec intact. Add a zero-scaled candidate-attention score over five causal input embeddings, conditioned on the SASRec state. It targets unsupported broad associations and overlooked entertainment distinctions; collaborative embeddings may lack those distinctions, and ownership or compatibility remains unknown.

Target the observed 17.4–24.0% training wrong-rank rate, especially Industrial_and_Scientific. Compare baseline and residual under unchanged training, then a matched candidate-independent control if budget permits. Pass immutable context explicitly; normalize attention over history slots, and verify candidate-chunk invariance. Select exclusively through pinned four-category macro validation HR@10. Conditional paired intervals remain exploratory after adaptive selection. No improvement or full-catalog superiority is claimed.
