ning and longer transition-count windows as deferred experiments unless a remaining-budget allowance was predeclared. Do not claim superiority over unrun controls. Avoid combining an objective change with this initial fusion test.\n\nTransition counts can exploit frequent items, repeated item pairs and collection patterns without capturing practical usefulness. Popularity-only, transition-only, last-item and order-insensitive controls expose some of these shortcuts. Stronger margins can also suppress plausible alternatives; use diagnostics only for explanation and select by the specified validation metric. Log every attempted checkpoint and configuration, including failures and cost. Repeated selection can overfit 800 validation users; keep this grid bounded and freeze the selected configuration before independent held-out evaluation. Never access test labels or hidden files, change splits or candidates, or modify the host evaluator.\n\nIf the existing evaluator exposes paired per-user hit indicators, bootstrap 10,000 paired resamples using an independent analysis RNG seeded 42. Resample 200 users with replacement independently within each category, use identical resampled indices for both models, and average the four category differences. Report the observed difference, category differences, discordant hit counts and percentile 95% intervals. Otherwise report aggregates without inventing uncertainty or changing the evaluator. One changed hit moves category HR by 0.5 percentage points and macro HR by 0.125 points. These intervals condition on fixed candidates and trained checkpoints; they exclude training-seed and candidate-sampling variability and do not correct winner-selection bias. Interpret HR@10 strictly on the pinned candidate distribution, not as full-catalog HR@10.\n\nUpdate the predictor writer so working/predict.py loads each category's SASRec checkpoint, sparse counts and row totals, item mapping, tau, history window, decay, frozen scales and selected coefficient. Preserve routing, unseen-item filtering, identical neural score conventions, and chunk-invariant candidate scoring. Retain full-catalog unseen-item recommendations for five real training users per category. Verify alpha=0 score parity, sparse-versus-dense formula parity on a tiny artificial count table, empty and unsupported histories, and unchanged rankings under candidate chunking or permutation. These are focused correctness checks, not empirical performance evidence.\n\nRetain DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis), returning measured metrics and metric_definitions. Keep view.model as the actual SASRec nn.Module and preserve item_emb, log2feats, predict_score, user_train, meta_data and review_data compatibility. Preserve loader tuples (None,sequence,positive,negative), with B x L tensors. Keep neural features and neural-only scores explicitly labeled; expose transition artifacts separately instead of representing them as an untrained neural architecture. The interpretation prompt must use the returned measured metrics and definitions and mark unavailable measurements explicitly.\n\nAdd a separate fused diagnostic only when actual source-prefix histories and frozen fusion artifacts are available; otherwise return null with an explicit reason. The loader tuple alone may not identify a unique original occurrence, particularly with repeated IDs or truncated histories. Use an explicit occurrence mapping from the unchanged training pipeline or a separate identified training-prefix sample; never reconstruct provenance by ambiguous item matching. For a queried adjacent occurrence b->a, temporarily subtract exactly one from C[b,a] and n[b] before scoring all retained sources with the adjusted global table. If a source ID repeats in the prefix, every lookup of that row uses the adjusted count. Keep calibration scales and floors frozen; do not recalibrate after subtraction. This model has n