INFO:self_evolverec:The new idea for diagnosis in iteration 4:
{
  "description": "Fuse each unchanged per-dataset SASRec predictor with a sparse, recency-weighted directed-transition residual and a separately weighted popularity residual. Freeze training-derived scales and compare a predeclared nine-setting factorial grid covering baseline, popularity alone, transitions alone, and both.",
  "motivation": "The supplied feedback motivates testing whether explicit recent item associations improve ranking. Existing diagnostics establish neither validation gains nor compatibility failures. FPMC, FOSSIL, and recent transition baselines establish strong prior art, making this a controlled empirical adaptation. Sharing neural artifacts and matching residual tuning help distinguish additional transition information from popularity effects, although they cannot establish causal user intent.",
  "implementation_notes": "Build separate statistics and mappings for Movies_and_TV, Industrial_and_Scientific, Electronics, and CDs_and_Vinyl. Use only adjacent pairs wholly inside each user's training history, preserving the baseline history order and excluding padding. Retain repeated events and self-transitions when present; never form pairs across users or include validation targets. Let C[a,i] count adjacent occurrences and out_count[a] sum the full unpruned row. Compute T[a,i]=C[a,i]/(out_count[a]+10), retain the largest 100 entries per row, and break ties by ascending item ID. Do not renormalize after pruning. This is a shrunk empirical transition score, not a calibrated probability distribution. Set qT to the nearest-rank 95th percentile of positive retained entries, or 1 when empty; replace entries with min(T/qT,1). Pool the last five nonpadding input items, including the final input item, with normalized weights 0.8**lag, where lag zero is most recent. Repeated source items retain repeated weight. Empty history returns zero. Define popularity from all training interaction occurrences as log1p(count), divide by the nearest-rank 95th percentile among positive-count catalog items, and clip to [0,1]; use scale 1 when empty and zero for unseen items. Padding always receives zero residual.\n\nAfter the baseline checkpoint is fixed, estimate neural scale a independently per category using CPU RNG seed 42. Sample up to 4,096 eligible training users uniformly without replacement in sorted user-ID order; for each, sample one source position uniformly, construct its causal prefix using the baseline truncation and padding convention, and sample two distinct nonpadding catalog IDs uniformly without excluding history. Compute RMS of their finite neural score differences in evaluation mode. Restore model mode afterward. Use a=1 if support is empty or the RMS is nonfinite or <=1e-8. Record support and fallback reasons. Freeze all scales before any fusion validation. Candidate-wise normalization is prohibited because scores must remain consistent across candidate panels and full-catalog chunks. Training-derived scales deliberately avoid additional validation fitting; this differs from the supplied suggestion to fit scales on validation. Scaling neural scores alone preserves rankings mathematically, but verify numerical parity with the unchanged baseline; the zero-residual configuration must invoke the original scorer directly for exact baseline behavior.\n\nPredeclare the global coefficient grid lambda_T,lambda_P in {0,0.1,0.3}; use the same pair across all four categories, with no per-category coefficient selection. Evaluate nine combinations using the same fixed neural checkpoints and pinned host candidates. Do not retrain SASRec for each fusion setting. Preserve the baseline checkpoint evaluation and selection schedule exactly, logging it before execution; any existing checkpoint selection also contributes validation search exposure. Select the completed setting with maximum four-category macro validation HR@10. Resolve exact ties by ascending (lambda_T+lambda_P, lambda_T, lambda_P), which favors baseline, smaller total residual weight, and popularity over transitions at equal total weight. Record every attempted configuration, all category outcomes, and incomplete evaluations. Nine settings increase selection risk relative to the original five; disclose this expansion and treat small gains as exploratory. Do not add adaptive coefficient, pruning, shrinkage, window, or calibration sweeps. Log-compressed transitions, support gating, and title backoff are separately budgeted follow-ups.\n\nKeep DATA_DIR=os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, SASREC_EPOCHS environment behavior with host default 200, single-GPU execution, unchanged splits and evaluator, all dataset routes, and trial-local artifacts. Serialize baseline architecture configuration and checkpoint, sparse matrices, popularity vectors, scales, mappings, selected coefficients, and tie rules. Update the predictor writer so working/predict.py reconstructs identical scoring for arbitrary candidate order, candidate subsets, batches, and chunked full-catalog recommendations. Retain baseline final-position conventions and seen-item filtering. Verify zero-residual equality, causal prefix alignment, padding and empty-history behavior, sparse lookup against a small dense calculation, candidate-subset/chunk invariance, and checkpoint/predictor parity before expensive evaluations.\n\nRetain DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). Keep view.model as the actual trained nn.Module and preserve item_emb, log2feats, predict_score, user_train, meta_data, and review_data compatibility. Neural features alone do not contain the sparse residual: add an explicit sequence-aware fused-scoring adapter and use it consistently in inference and fused probes. For training position t, score from sequence[:, :t+1], never the full sequence. Return measured metrics and metric_definitions, report support, and restore model mode in finally. Keep neural and fused margins distinct. Mark unsupported evidence unavailable rather than zero; leave category lists empty and do not consume ratings, reviews, prices, stores, or inferred simulator traits.\n\nMeasure zero-transition frequency, nonzero residual quantiles, source/edge support, scaled component contributions, and top-10 entries/exits on explicitly identified diagnostic panels. Report component-removal results with coefficients and scales frozen as model sensitivity, separately from retrained comparisons. Training transition diagnostics otherwise self-count observed edges: subtract the queried user's entire training contribution from C, outgoing totals, and popularity counts before rebuilding that diagnostic user's sparse rows, using frozen original scales. Recompute top-100 rows from full counts after subtraction, not 